  "text": "The study looked at how a breakage‑based method for estimating the size of grains in a high‑temperature phase of steel compares with other techniques. It was discovered that this breakage method does not give reliable grain‑size values for steels that have a very low carbon content (under about 0.4%). The same method also proved unsuitable for steels that have been surface‑enriched with carbon through a diffusion process. Additionally, when the grain size was measured using either the breakage approach or a rapid‑cooling‑twice procedure, the results were consistently smaller than those obtained with a conventional optical‑microscopy based test. These findings suggest that the breakage and double‑cooling techniques underestimate the true grain dimensions in these types of alloy steels."
